micro_lesson.content:
A runtime error occurs when the program is running, not when it is being parsed. With dictionaries, the most common runtime errors are KeyError (accessing a missing key) and TypeError (using an unhashable type as a key). Another subtle runtime issue is modifying a dictionary while iterating over it, which can cause unexpected behavior or crashes depending on the Python version. Always iterate over a copy of the keys if you plan to delete or modify entries during traversal. Here is an example of a safe deletion pattern:

```python
scores = {'alice': 10, 'bob': 20, 'carol': 15}
keys_to_remove = [k for k, v in scores.items() if v < 18]
for k in keys_to_remove:
    del scores[k]
print(scores)  # Output: {'alice': 10, 'bob': 20}
```
Notice we iterate over keys_to_remove, a separate list, rather than scores directly.

